      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-intersection-collision">
    <title>Intersection Points versus Collision Points</title>

    <p>
      A parametrization carries more information than the curve it traces: it
      also records <em>when</em> the moving point visits each place on the
      curve. Because of this, there are two different questions we can ask
      about two parametric curves

    <md>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{r}_1(t) \amp = \left\langle f_1(t),\, g_1(t)\right\rangle,
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{r}_2(t) \amp = \left\langle f_2(t),\, g_2(t)\right\rangle,
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      thought of as the trajectories of two particles.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-intersection-collision">
      <title>Intersection Points and Collision Points</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A point <m>P</m> is an <term>intersection point</term> of the two
          curves if <m>P</m> lies on both curves; that is, if there are
          parameter values <m>t</m> and <m>s</m>, not necessarily equal, with

        <md>
          \mathbf{r}_1(t) = \overrightarrow{OP} = \mathbf{r}_2(s).
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          A point <m>P</m> is a <term>collision point</term> of the two
          particles if both particles are at <m>P</m> at the same time; that
          is, if there is a single value of <m>t</m> with

        <md>
          \mathbf{r}_1(t) = \mathbf{r}_2(t) = \overrightarrow{OP}.
        </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      An intersection point is a statement about the two <em>paths</em>: they
      cross, like two roads on a map. A collision point is a statement about
      the two <em>motions</em>: the cars are at the crossroads at the same
      instant.
    </p>

    <exercise xml:id="check-collision-vs-intersection">
      <title>True or False</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Decide whether each statement is true or false.
        </p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              Every collision point of two particles is an intersection
              point of their paths.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Every intersection point of two paths is a collision point of
              the particles that travel along them.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {(a) true or false, and why:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.2) {(b) true or false, and why:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for each statement, for the verdict and the reason.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="check-intersection-collision-equations">
      <title>True or False</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          In practice the distinction dictates how we set up the equations.
          Decide whether each statement is true or false.
        </p>
        <ol marker="(a)">
          <li>
            <p>
              To find the <term>intersection points</term> of the curves
              <m>\mathbf{r}_1(t) = \left\langle f_1(t), g_1(t)\right\rangle</m>
              and
              <m>\mathbf{r}_2(t) = \left\langle f_2(t), g_2(t)\right\rangle</m>,
              give the two curves different parameter names and solve
              <m>f_1(t) = f_2(s)</m>, <m>g_1(t) = g_2(s)</m> for the pair
              <m>(t,s)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              To find the <term>collision points</term> of the two
              particles, eliminate the parameters and intersect the two
              Cartesian curves.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,2.5) -- (15,2.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.7) {(a) true or false, and why:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.2) {(b) true or false, and why:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for each statement, for the verdict and the reason.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <example xml:id="ex-paths-cross-no-collision">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Two particles move in the plane with position vectors

        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_1(t) \amp = \left\langle t,\; t^2\right\rangle,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_2(t) \amp = \left\langle -t,\; 2-t\right\rangle,
                                   \qquad t \in \R .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Find all intersection points of their paths, and all collision
          points of the particles.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,13);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9) -- (15,9);
\draw[gray!35] (0,4.5) -- (15,4.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,12.7) {the two paths and where they meet:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,8.7) {collision equations:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,4.2) {when each particle reaches each crossing:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the two paths after eliminating the parameter and the points where they meet, for the collision equations, and for the times at which each particle reaches each crossing.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </example>
        <figure xml:id="fig-intersection-no-collision">
          <caption>
            The parabola <m>C_1</m> and the line <m>C_2</m>; compare with
            what you found.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-intersection-no-collision" width="75%">
            <shortdescription>
              A parabola labelled C one and a line labelled C two crossing at
              two points, each marked with a dot.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                The parabola y equals x squared, labelled C one, and the line
                y equals x plus two, labelled C two, drawn on the same
                coordinate grid with arrows showing the direction of travel.
                Their two crossings are marked with red dots and are not
                labelled.
              </p>
            </description>
            <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                       label="intersection-no-collision">
              <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                <definition>p(t) = (t, t^2)</definition>
                <definition>l(t) = (-t, 2-t)</definition>
                <coordinates bbox="(-4.6,-1.8,5.6,7.2)">
                  <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>

                  <group at="parabola">
                    <!-- the arrow sits well up the right branch: at
                         t=2 it would land under the crossing point -->
                    <parametric-curve function="p" domain="(-2.4,2.6)"
                                      stroke="blue" thickness="3"
                                      arrows="1" arrow-location="2.45"/>
                    <label anchor="p(-2.3)" alignment="nw" offset="(-6,4)"
                           color="blue">
                      <m>C_1</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <group at="line">
                    <parametric-curve function="l" domain="(-3.4,3.1)"
                                      stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"
                                      arrows="1" arrow-location="2"/>
                    <label anchor="l(-2.9)" alignment="se" offset="(8,-2)"
                           color="#009E73">
                      <m>C_2</m>
                    </label>
                  </group>

                  <!-- The crossings are marked but not labelled: the
                       coordinates and the arrival times are what the box
                       above asks for. -->
                  <group at="crossings">
                    <point p="(-1,1)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                    <point p="(2,4)" size="4.5" stroke="red" fill="red"/>
                  </group>
                </coordinates>

                <annotations>
                  <annotation ref="figure"
                              text="A parabola and a line crossing at two
                                    points that the two particles reach at
                                    different times, so there is no collision">
                    <annotation ref="grid" text="The coordinate grid"/>
                    <annotation ref="axes" text="The coordinate axes"/>
                    <annotation ref="parabola"
                                text="The path of the first particle, the
                                      parabola y equals x squared"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="line"
                                text="The path of the second particle, the
                                      line y equals x plus two"
                                sonify="yes"/>
                    <annotation ref="crossings"
                                text="The two intersection points, marked
                                      with dots"/>
                  </annotation>
                </annotations>
              </diagram>
            </prefigure>
          </image>
        </figure>

    <example xml:id="ex-same-paths-collision">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep the first particle, but re-schedule the second one:

        <md>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_1(t) \amp = \left\langle t,\; t^2\right\rangle,
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \mathbf{r}_2(t) \amp = \left\langle 2t-2,\; 2t\right\rangle,
                                   \qquad t \in \R .
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Find the intersection points of the paths and the collision points
          of the particles.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="46% 46%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (7,10);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6.7) -- (7,6.7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.4) -- (7,3.4);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,9.7) {the paths:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,6.4) {collision equations:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.3,3.1) {the other crossing:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows, for the paths, for the collision computation, and for what happens at the other crossing.</shortdescription>
          </image>
          <image>
            <latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55, >=stealth]
\draw[gray!30, very thin] (-4,-2) grid (4,6);
\draw[->, thick] (-4,0) -- (4.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-2) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x in {-3,-2,-1,1,2,3} \draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12) node[below=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\x$};
\foreach \y in {-1,1,2,3,4,5} \draw (-0.12,\y) -- (0.12,\y) node[left=4pt, font=\scriptsize] {$\y$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Empty coordinate grid running from negative four to four horizontally and negative two to six vertically, for drawing the parabola and the line and marking the collision point.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <exercise xml:id="check-collision-both-points">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep <m>\mathbf{r}_1(t) = \left\langle t,t^2\right\rangle</m>. Find a parametrization
          <m>\mathbf{r}_2</m> of the line <m>y = x+2</m> for which the two
          particles collide at <em>both</em> intersection points.
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$\mathbf{r}_2(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {the collision equations it produces:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in two rows, one for the new parametrization of the line and one for the collision equations it produces.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-curves-in-space">
    <title>Curves in Space and Vector Functions</title>

    <p>
      Assume that we are interested in describing the path of an object in
      space. To this aim, we can specify its location at each instant of
      time; that is, we can write its coordinates in <m>\R^3</m> as

    <md>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-space-parametric" number="yes">
        x = f(t), \qquad y = g(t), \qquad z = h(t).
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Here <m>t</m> is the time parameter, which varies from some initial
      time <m>t_i</m> to some final time <m>t_f</m>, that is,
      <m>t \in [t_i, t_f]</m>. Nothing has changed, except that where a
      plane curve needed the two equations
      <xref ref="eq-parametric-equations"/>, a space curve needs three.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is a second, more compact way to look at
      <xref ref="eq-space-parametric"/>, and it is the point of view we will
      use for the rest of the course. Instead of three separate coordinate
      equations, collect the coordinates into a single
      <term>vector function</term> <mdash/> a function whose input is a real
      number and whose output is a vector:

    <md>
      <mrow>
        \mathbf{r}(t) \amp = \left\langle f(t),\ g(t),\ h(t)
                             \right\rangle
      </mrow>
      <mrow xml:id="eq-vector-function" number="yes">
        \amp = f(t)\,\mathbf{i} + g(t)\,\mathbf{j} + h(t)\,\mathbf{k},
               \qquad t \in [t_i,t_f].
      </mrow>
    </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Think of <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> as the <term>position vector</term> of
      the moving object: an arrow drawn from the origin to the object's
      location at time <m>t</m>. As <m>t</m> increases, the arrow swings
      around and changes length, and its <em>tip</em> sweeps out the curve.
      So a parametrization can be read in two equivalent ways.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Coordinatewise.</term> Three ordinary scalar functions
          <m>f</m>, <m>g</m>, <m>h</m> of one variable, one for each
          coordinate, as in <xref ref="eq-space-parametric"/>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <term>Vectorially.</term> One vector-valued function
          <m>\mathbf{r}</m> of one variable, as in
          <xref ref="eq-vector-function"/>. The curve is the set of tips of
          the vectors <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The two descriptions carry exactly the same information, but the vector
      form is the more useful one: it lets us differentiate and integrate a
      whole motion at once, and its derivative will turn out to be the
      velocity of the moving object. The same device works in the plane,
      where <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle f(t), g(t)\right\rangle</m>; the
      position vectors <m>\mathbf{r}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{r}_2</m> of
      <xref ref="ex-paths-cross-no-collision"/> were already written this
      way.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-helix-space-curve">
      <title>A helix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the object whose coordinates are parametrized by

        <md>
          <mrow>
            x \amp = \cos(2t), \qquad y = \sin(2t),
          </mrow>
          <mrow xml:id="eq-helix-param-space" number="yes">
            z \amp = t, \qquad t \in [0,\pi].
          </mrow>
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Write the motion as a vector function and describe the path.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,6) -- (15,6);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3) -- (15,3);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$\mathbf{r}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {the shadow on the $xy$-plane, and how often it goes around:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,5.7) {what $z$ does:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,2.7) {the path, its start, and its end:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the vector function, for the shadow of the path on the x y plane, for the behaviour of the height, and for the name of the curve with its starting and ending points.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <figure xml:id="fig-helix-space-curve">
          <caption>
            One turn of the helix
            <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle \cos(2t), \sin(2t), t
            \right\rangle</m>, <m>0 \le t \le \pi</m>. The violet arrow is
            the position vector <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> at
            <m>t = 3\pi/8</m>; its tip rides along the curve as <m>t</m>
            increases. The dashed gray circle is the shadow of the path on
            the plane <m>z=0</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-helix-space-curve" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
              One turn of a helix rising above the unit circle, with the
              position vector drawn from the origin to a point on the curve.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
                minus 1.3 to 1.3 and z from 0 to about 3.4. A thick blue curve
                spirals counterclockwise once around the vertical axis while
                climbing steadily, starting at a red point labeled (1,0,0) at
                time t equals zero on the floor of the box and ending at a red
                point labeled (1,0,pi) at time t equals pi, directly above the
                start. A dashed gray unit circle on the floor is the shadow of
                the path. A violet arrow labeled r of t runs from the origin
                to a point partway up the curve, illustrating the position
                vector whose tip traces the helix.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  width=10cm, view={115}{18},
                  xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                  xmin=-1.3, xmax=1.3, ymin=-1.3, ymax=1.3,
                  zmin=0, zmax=3.4,
                  grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                ]
                % shadow of the path on the plane z=0
                \addplot3[gray!65, dashed, line width=1.3pt, domain=0:180,
                          samples=120, samples y=0]
                    ({cos(2*x)}, {sin(2*x)}, {0});
                % the helix itself
                \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:180,
                          samples=200, samples y=0]
                    ({cos(2*x)}, {sin(2*x)}, {pi*x/180});
                % the position vector r(t) at t = 3pi/8
                \addplot3[-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]}, violet, thick]
                    coordinates {(0,0,0) (-0.70711,0.70711,1.17810)};
                \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, violet, mark size=1.8pt]
                    coordinates {(-0.70711,0.70711,1.17810)};
                % start and end of the motion
                \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.8pt]
                    coordinates {(1,0,0) (1,0,3.14159)};
                \node[left=4pt, yshift=7pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0)
                    {\((1,0,0),\ t=0\)};
                \node[above right] at (axis cs:1,0,3.14159)
                    {\((1,0,\pi),\ t=\pi\)};
                \node[violet, above left=-2pt]
                    at (axis cs:-0.35355,0.35355,0.58905)
                    {\(\mathbf{r}(t)\)};
              \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>

        <p>
          The same helix, animated. Press <q>Play</q> to watch the point
          climb the curve, with its shadow running around the unit circle
          below and the position vector <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> following it;
          or drag the slider to place the point yourself.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive-helix-manim" iframe="helix-manim.html"
                     width="100%" aspect="1:1">
          <instructions>
            <p>
              Drag <m>t</m> from <m>0</m> to <m>\pi</m>, or use the
              <q>Jump to</q> buttons. The card reads off the point
              <m>(\cos 2t, \sin 2t, t)</m>, and the readout at the lower
              left keeps track of the height <m>z = t</m> and of how far
              around the circle the shadow has gone. Drag the figure to
              look from anywhere, <q>Spin</q> turns it on its own, and
              <q>Full screen</q> fills the screen with it.
            </p>
          </instructions>
        </interactive>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      There are situations in which a curve in space arises as the
      intersection of two surfaces. In the next two examples we turn such a
      description into a vector function <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m>.
    </p>

    <example xml:id="ex-cylinder-plane">
      <title>Where a plane cuts a cylinder</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find a vector function that represents the curve of intersection of
          the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> and the plane
          <m>y + z = 2</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\draw[gray!35] (0,9.5) -- (15,9.5);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,11.7) {$x(t)$ and $y(t)$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,9.2) {$z(t)$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$\mathbf{r}(t) =$};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {check at $t = 0,\ \pi/2,\ \pi,\ 3\pi/2$:};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in four rows, for the x and y coordinates, for z, for the assembled vector function with the range of t, and for the check at four parameter values.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <figure xml:id="fig-cylinder-plane">
          <caption>
            The cylinder <m>x^2+y^2=1</m>, the plane <m>y+z=2</m>, and the
            ellipse <m>C</m> in which they meet. The violet arrow is the
            position vector <m>\mathbf{r}(t)</m> of your parametrization at
            <m>t=\pi/4</m>.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-cylinder-plane" width="70%">
            <shortdescription>
              A vertical cylinder cut by a slanted plane, with the ellipse
              where they meet drawn on the surfaces.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A three-dimensional plot showing a vertical circular cylinder
                of radius one standing on the xy-plane, and a slanted plane
                cutting through it. The two meet along a closed curve, an
                ellipse, drawn as a thick magenta loop and labeled C. Four
                points on the ellipse are marked, at (1,0,2), (0,1,1),
                (-1,0,2) and (0,-1,3). A violet arrow runs from the origin to
                a point on the ellipse, illustrating the position vector r of
                t.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  width=10.5cm, view={120}{16},
                  xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                  xmin=-1.9, xmax=1.9, ymin=-1.9, ymax=1.9,
                  zmin=0, zmax=3.6,
                  grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                ]
                % the cylinder x^2 + y^2 = 1
                \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.30, draw=gray!45,
                          colormap={cylgray}{color=(gray!15) color=(gray!45)},
                          domain=0:360, y domain=0:3.2,
                          samples=48, samples y=2]
                    ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {y});
                % the plane y + z = 2
                \draw[blue!55, fill=blue!25, fill opacity=0.30]
                    (axis cs:-1.9,-1.6,3.6) -- (axis cs:1.9,-1.6,3.6)
                    -- (axis cs:1.9,1.9,0.1) -- (axis cs:-1.9,1.9,0.1)
                    -- cycle;
                % the curve of intersection
                \addplot3[magenta, very thick, domain=0:360,
                          samples=180, samples y=0]
                    ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {2-sin(x)});
                % the position vector r(pi/4)
                \addplot3[-{Stealth[length=2.5mm]}, violet, thick]
                    coordinates {(0,0,0) (0.70711,0.70711,1.29289)};
                \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, black, mark size=1.6pt]
                    coordinates {(1,0,2) (0,1,1) (-1,0,2) (0,-1,3)};
                \node[right=1pt] at (axis cs:1,0,2) {\((1,0,2)\)};
                \node[below=1pt] at (axis cs:0,1,1) {\((0,1,1)\)};
                \node[left=1pt] at (axis cs:-1,0,2) {\((-1,0,2)\)};
                \node[above=1pt] at (axis cs:0,-1,3) {\((0,-1,3)\)};
                \node[magenta, above left=-2pt]
                    at (axis cs:-0.70711,-0.70711,2.70711) {\(C\)};
                \node[violet, above left=-2pt]
                    at (axis cs:0.35355,0.35355,0.64645)
                    {\(\mathbf{r}(t)\)};
              \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-two-surfaces">
      <title>Where two curved surfaces meet</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Parametrize the intersection of the surfaces

        <md>
          z = \sqrt{1 + x^2 + y^2}
          \qquad \text{and} \qquad
          z = \sqrt{4 - 4x^2 - y^2} .
        </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Solution.</em>
        </p>

        <sidebyside width="97%">
          <image>
            <latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,14);
\draw[gray!35] (0,7) -- (15,7);
\draw[gray!35] (0,3.5) -- (15,3.5);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,6.7) {$x(t)$ and $y(t)$:};
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small\itshape] at (0.35,3.2) {$z(t)$, and $\mathbf{r}(t) =$};
\end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
            <shortdescription>Blank box in three rows: an unlabelled one for finding the shadow of the curve, one for the parametrization of the shadow, and one for z of t with the assembled vector function.</shortdescription>
          </image>
        </sidebyside>
        <figure xml:id="fig-two-surfaces">
          <caption>
            The upper sheet of <m>z = \sqrt{1+x^2+y^2}</m> (blue) and the
            dome <m>z = \sqrt{4-4x^2-y^2}</m> (red) meet along the closed
            curve drawn in black. Its shadow in the plane <m>z=0</m> is the
            dashed ellipse; compare it with the equation you found.
          </caption>
          <image xml:id="image-two-surfaces" width="100%">
            <shortdescription>
              A bowl-shaped surface and a dome overlapping, with the closed
              curve where they meet drawn on them.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A three-dimensional plot showing two surfaces. The first, in
                blue, is the upper sheet of a hyperboloid opening upward from
                its lowest point at height one above the origin. The second,
                in red, is a dome, the upper half of an ellipsoid, reaching
                height two above the origin. The two surfaces cross along a
                closed curve, drawn in black, which loops around the vertical
                axis at heights between about 1.26 and 1.58. A dashed ellipse
                on the floor of the box shows the shadow of that curve.
              </p>
            </description>
            <latex-image>
              \begin{tikzpicture}
              \begin{axis}[
                  width=10.5cm, view={120}{16},
                  xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                  xmin=-1.6, xmax=1.6, ymin=-2.2, ymax=2.2,
                  zmin=0, zmax=2.4,
                  grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
                  % the surfaces are named in a legend rather than on the plot
                  legend style={at={(1.02,1)}, anchor=north west,
                                draw=gray!45, fill=white, font=\small,
                                cells={anchor=west}, row sep=2pt},
                  legend cell align=left,
                ]
                % the dome z = sqrt(4 - 4x^2 - y^2)
                \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.50, draw=red!55,
                          colormap={domered}{color=(red!25) color=(red!55)},
                          domain=0:90, y domain=0:360,
                          samples=16, samples y=48]
                    ({sin(x)*cos(y)}, {2*sin(x)*sin(y)}, {2*cos(x)});
                % the upper sheet z = sqrt(1 + x^2 + y^2)
                \addplot3[surf, shader=flat, opacity=0.50, draw=blue!55,
                          colormap={sheetblue}{color=(blue!25) color=(blue!55)},
                          domain=0:1.5, y domain=0:360,
                          samples=14, samples y=48]
                    ({x*cos(y)}, {x*sin(y)}, {sqrt(1+x^2)});
                % shadow of the intersection curve
                \addplot3[gray!70, dashed, line width=2pt, domain=0:360,
                          samples=120, samples y=0]
                    ({0.774597*cos(x)}, {1.224745*sin(x)}, {0});
                % the curve of intersection
                \addplot3[black, very thick, domain=0:360,
                          samples=180, samples y=0]
                    ({0.774597*cos(x)}, {1.224745*sin(x)},
                     {sqrt(1+0.6*(cos(x))^2+1.5*(sin(x))^2)});
                % One entry per plot, in the order the plots were drawn:
                % dome, sheet, shadow, curve.  (An \addlegendentry binds to
                % the next plot without a label, so the order matters.)
                \legend{\(z=\sqrt{4-4x^2-y^2}\),
                        \(z=\sqrt{1+x^2+y^2}\),
                        shadow of the curve\, \(z=0\),
                        curve of intersection}
              \end{axis}
              \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
          </image>
        </figure>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>

</section>
